## 9e06cd05 Take a turn's late stop for no part of the next turn

terminal-host-stop.test.ts "tells Hornhoof how full each agent's context is"
failed once on a loaded machine, and the defect is in the product. The host
answers a turn as soon as it reads the end in the program's log, so Hornhoof's
next turn can begin before the program's stop hook of that same end arrives.
stoppedHook applied that hook to whatever ran at its arrival.

When the next turn starts a new program, the late stop made the host follow
the old session back and read its log from the start. That log's own end then
ended the new turn: host.run answered the next prompt with the last turn's
answer and session, before the program had taken the prompt, and a context
reading was lost. In the same session the late stop held the next turn for
SETTLE_MS and could end it as done.

Reproduced on home-api under a CPU load, on the old code: 4 of 127 rounds lost
the reading, each with a stop that arrived after the first turn was answered,
and each answered "echo: hello" to the second prompt.

A stop from before the running turn took its prompt, or from a session the
agent left, now only shows whether the two channels disagree. stopIsLate is a
pure function with direct tests; two integration tests deliver the stop at a
chosen point of the next turn, with no load and no sleeps.

Ruling: a stop before the running turn's prompt went in is never that turn's, read after draining the log - why: a program takes one prompt at a time, and the log's turn.prompt counts for a lost prompt hook - cost if wrong: a real stop that beats both its prompt hook and its log line is ignored, and the turn ends from the log instead.
Ruling: a stop from the session the agent is in, after the next prompt went in, still counts for the running turn - why: Kimi's Stop names no turn, and both fakes wait for the stop's answer before they take the next prompt, so a program in one session cannot do this - cost if wrong: if real Kimi takes a prompt while its last stop is still unanswered, that turn ends early as done after SETTLE_MS.

